63.821, 64.413]}, "text": "So where does that leave us? Start with what is solid. The Greeks told at least three different Cyclops stories, and kept them apart. A single eye is stated by Hesiod, Euripides, and Thee-ockritus. Homer implies it. Dwarf elephants really lived on those islands, and their skulls really do have a hole in the middle. The blinding story itself is a folktale. Scholars have counted over two hundred versions, from Iceland to Korea, and most think the tale is older than Homer. That does not rule out the skull. It does mean the story does not need one. Now, what we cannot say. No surviving ancient text says anyone mistook a skull for a Cyclops. Other explanations compete: volcanic craters, smith guilds, even a birth defect called cyclopia, though in that condition the eye sits below the nose, not above it. Ah-gheereh and Buxton's verdict is that each has an air of plausibility, and none has proof. The skull theory is a hypothesis. A plausible one, and still unproven.", "orig": "So where does that leave us? Start with what is solid. The Greeks told at least three different Cyclops stories, and kept them apart. A single eye is stated by Hesiod, Euripides, and Theocritus. Homer implies it. Dwarf elephants really lived on those islands, and their skulls really do have a hole in the middle. The blinding story itself is a folktale. Scholars have counted over two hundred versions, from Iceland to Korea, and most think the tale is older than Homer. That does not rule out the skull. It does mean the story does not need one. Now, what we cannot say. No surviving ancient text says anyone mistook a skull for a Cyclops. Other explanations compete: volcanic craters, smith guilds, even a birth defect called cyclopia, though in that condition the eye sits below the nose, not above it. Aguirre and Buxton's verdict is that each has an air of plausibility, and none has proof. The skull theory is a hypothesis. A plausible one, and still unproven."}